#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: ggdef
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [cow, borrow, copy]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #11 (Copy axis, ACCEPT direction). `bump(&p, p.b)` passes a mutable
#   borrow `&p` AND a bare read of the field `p.b` in the SAME call. Their places
#   overlap (`p` vs `p.p`), but `p.b` is `int` — a Copy type — so the bare read is
#   a VALUE SNAPSHOT taken before the borrow's exclusive write, not a live view
#   that conflicts (D10(b) place-overlap: a Copy-typed bare read is exempt). Both
#   compilers ACCEPT and run; the loop-free body computes `p.a = 3 + 4 = 7`. This
#   is the POS complement of reject_borrow_conflict_noncopy_field.gg — flipping the
#   field to a non-Copy type turns the same shape into an E_BorrowConflict. The
#   Copy extent mirrors production `is_copy_type` exactly (scalar / tuple-of-Copy /
#   all-Copy-field struct). NOTE: an int field is `Ty::Prim`, the Copy axis's
#   scalar BASE case — the pre-fix Prim-only oracle already accepted this exact
#   program, so this fixture guards only that base case. The pin on the #11
#   divergence (the STRUCT extension the old axis mis-modelled) is
#   copy_struct_field_borrow_ok.gg, whose all-int struct-field read the Prim-only
#   axis rejects (counterfactual-verified).
# expect:
#   exit: 0
#   stdout: "7\n"
#!end

struct Pair:
    int a
    int b

void bump(Pair &p, int extra):
    p.a = p.a + extra

void main():
    Pair p = Pair(3, 4)
    bump(&p, p.b)
    print(p.a)
